[{"input":"# Lecture 7 – Operating Systems\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain why operating systems exist.\n- Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.\n- Distinguish between applications and the operating system.\n- Identify major operating system families.\n- Understand the concepts of processes and threads.\n- Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.\n- Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.\n- Explain the purpose of device drivers.\n- Understand how file systems organize data.\n- Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapters, we learned about:\n- Computer hardware\n- Machine instructions\n- Programming\n- Software development\n\nA natural question now arises:\n\n> How do all these software applications share a single computer?\n\nConsider a typical laptop.\n\nAt the same time, it may be running:\n- A web browser\n- A music player\n- A word processor\n- Antivirus software\n- Several background services\n\nHow is this possible?\n\nThe answer is the **Operating System**.\n\nThe operating system acts as the manager of the computer.\n\n## What Is an Operating System?\n\nAn Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.\n\nExamples include:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Position in the Computing Stack\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nApplications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.\n\n### Main Responsibilities\n\nAn operating system manages:\n- Processors\n- Memory\n- Files\n- Storage\n- Network connections\n- Devices\n- Security\n\nWithout an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.\n\n## Life Without an Operating System\n\nTo appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.\n\nA programmer would need to:\n- Manage memory manually\n- Communicate directly with devices\n- Control storage hardware\n- Handle keyboard and screen operations\n- Coordinate multiple programs\n\nEvery application would repeat the same work.\n\n### Problems\n\nWithout an operating system:\n- Software would become extremely complex.\n- Applications could easily interfere with each other.\n- Security would be poor.\n- Hardware compatibility would be difficult.\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a large office building.\nWithout management:\n- Rooms are assigned randomly.\n- Resources are shared inefficiently.\n- Conflicts occur constantly.\n\nThe operating system acts as the building manager.","output":"","result":"# Lecture 7 – Operating Systems\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain why operating systems exist.\n- Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.\n- Distinguish between applications and the operating system.\n- Identify major operating system families.\n- Understand the concepts of processes and threads.\n- Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.\n- Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.\n- Explain the purpose of device drivers.\n- Understand how file systems organize data.\n- Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapters, we learned about:\n- Computer hardware\n- Machine instructions\n- Programming\n- Software development\n\nA natural question now arises:\n\n> How do all these software applications share a single computer?\n\nConsider a typical laptop.\n\nAt the same time, it may be running:\n- A web browser\n- A music player\n- A word processor\n- Antivirus software\n- Several background services\n\nHow is this possible?\n\nThe answer is the **Operating System**.\n\nThe operating system acts as the manager of the computer.\n\n## What Is an Operating System?\n\nAn Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.\n\nExamples include:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Position in the Computing Stack\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nApplications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.\n\n### Main Responsibilities\n\nAn operating system manages:\n- Processors\n- Memory\n- Files\n- Storage\n- Network connections\n- Devices\n- Security\n\nWithout an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.\n\n## Life Without an Operating System\n\nTo appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.\n\nA programmer would need to:\n- Manage memory manually\n- Communicate directly with devices\n- Control storage hardware\n- Handle keyboard and screen operations\n- Coordinate multiple programs\n\nEvery application would repeat the same work.\n\n### Problems\n\nWithout an operating system:\n- Software would become extremely complex.\n- Applications could easily interfere with each other.\n- Security would be poor.\n- Hardware compatibility would be difficult.\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a large office building.\nWithout management:\n- Rooms are assigned randomly.\n- Resources are shared inefficiently.\n- Conflicts occur constantly.\n\nThe operating system acts as the building manager.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-lecture-7-operating-systems\">\n<h1>Lecture 7 – Operating Systems</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain why operating systems exist.</li>\n<li>Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.</li>\n<li>Distinguish between applications and the operating system.</li>\n<li>Identify major operating system families.</li>\n<li>Understand the concepts of processes and threads.</li>\n<li>Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.</li>\n<li>Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.</li>\n<li>Explain the purpose of device drivers.</li>\n<li>Understand how file systems organize data.</li>\n<li>Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>In the previous chapters, we learned about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Computer hardware</li>\n<li>Machine instructions</li>\n<li>Programming</li>\n<li>Software development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A natural question now arises:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do all these software applications share a single computer?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Consider a typical laptop.</p>\n<p>At the same time, it may be running:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A web browser</li>\n<li>A music player</li>\n<li>A word processor</li>\n<li>Antivirus software</li>\n<li>Several background services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How is this possible?</p>\n<p>The answer is the <strong>Operating System</strong>.</p>\n<p>The operating system acts as the manager of the computer.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-what-is-an-operating-system\">\n<h1>What Is an Operating System?</h1>\n<p>An Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows</li>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>macOS</li>\n<li>Android</li>\n<li>iOS</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-position-in-the-computing-stack\">\n<h1>Position in the Computing Stack</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">User\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware</pre>\n<p>Applications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-main-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Main Responsibilities</h1>\n<p>An operating system manages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Processors</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n<li>Files</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n<li>Network connections</li>\n<li>Devices</li>\n<li>Security</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-life-without-an-operating-system\">\n<h1>Life Without an Operating System</h1>\n<p>To appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.</p>\n<p>A programmer would need to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manage memory manually</li>\n<li>Communicate directly with devices</li>\n<li>Control storage hardware</li>\n<li>Handle keyboard and screen operations</li>\n<li>Coordinate multiple programs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every application would repeat the same work.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-problems\">\n<h1>Problems</h1>\n<p>Without an operating system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Software would become extremely complex.</li>\n<li>Applications could easily interfere with each other.</li>\n<li>Security would be poor.</li>\n<li>Hardware compatibility would be difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Think of a large office building.\nWithout management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rooms are assigned randomly.</li>\n<li>Resources are shared inefficiently.</li>\n<li>Conflicts occur constantly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The operating system acts as the building manager.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"91bc348f917b80f98084c22ed142838e5b2fb908ce35641484b1bd078112aa95","markdown":"# Les 7 – Besturingssystemen\n\n## Leerdoelen\n\nAan het einde van dit hoofdstuk moeten studenten in staat zijn om:\n- Uit te leggen waarom besturingssystemen bestaan.\n- De belangrijkste verantwoordelijkheden van een besturingssysteem te beschrijven.\n- Een onderscheid te maken tussen toepassingen en het besturingssysteem.\n- Belangrijke families van besturingssystemen te identificeren.\n- De concepten processen en threads te begrijpen.\n- Virtueel geheugen op conceptueel niveau uit te leggen.\n- De rol van API's, systeemaanroepen en bibliotheken te begrijpen.\n- Het doel van apparaatstuurprogramma's uit te leggen.\n- Te begrijpen hoe bestandssystemen gegevens organiseren.\n- Een onderscheid te maken tussen gebruikersmodus en kernelmodus.\n\n## Inleiding\n\nIn de voorgaande hoofdstukken hebben we geleerd over:\n- Computerhardware\n- Machine-instructies\n- Programmeren\n- Softwareontwikkeling\n\nEr rijst nu een natuurlijke vraag:\n\n> Hoe delen al deze softwaretoepassingen één enkele computer?\n\nDenk aan een typische laptop.\n\nTegelijkertijd kan hij draaien:\n- Een webbrowser\n- Een muziekspeler\n- Een tekstverwerker\n- Antivirussoftware\n- Verschillende achtergrondservices\n\nHoe is dit mogelijk?\n\nHet antwoord is het **Besturingssysteem**.\n\nHet besturingssysteem fungeert als de beheerder van de computer.\n\n## Wat Is een Besturingssysteem?\n\nEen besturingssysteem (OS) is software die computerbronnen beheert en diensten levert aan toepassingen.\n\nVoorbeelden zijn:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Positie in de Computerstack\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nToepassingen communiceren met het besturingssysteem, dat op zijn beurt communiceert met de hardware.\n\n### Belangrijkste Verantwoordelijkheden\n\nEen besturingssysteem beheert:\n- Processoren\n- Geheugen\n- Bestanden\n- Opslag\n- Netwerkverbindingen\n- Apparaten\n- Beveiliging\n\nZonder een besturingssysteem zou elke toepassing de hardware rechtstreeks moeten aansturen.\n\n## Leven Zonder een Besturingssysteem\n\nOm de rol van een besturingssysteem te waarderen, stel je een computer zonder besturingssysteem voor.\n\nEen programmeur zou het volgende moeten doen:\n- Geheugen handmatig beheren\n- Rechtstreeks communiceren met apparaten\n- Opslaghardware aansturen\n- Toetsenbord- en schermbewerkingen afhandelen\n- Meerdere programma's coördineren\n\nElke toepassing zou hetzelfde werk herhalen.\n\n### Problemen\n\nZonder een besturingssysteem:\n- Zou software extreem complex worden.\n- Zouden toepassingen elkaar gemakkelijk kunnen verstoren.\n- Zou de beveiliging zwak zijn.\n- Zou hardwarecompatibiliteit moeilijk zijn.\n\n### Analogie\n\nDenk aan een groot kantoorgebouw.\nZonder beheer:\n- Worden kamers willekeurig toegewezen.\n- Worden middelen inefficiënt gedeeld.\n- Ontstaan er voortdurend conflicten.\n\nHet besturingssysteem fungeert als de gebouwbeheerder."},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"91bc348f917b80f98084c22ed142838e5b2fb908ce35641484b1bd078112aa95","markdown":"# Cours 7 – Systèmes d'exploitation\n\n## Objectifs d'apprentissage\n\nÀ la fin de ce chapitre, les étudiants devraient être capables de :\n- Expliquer pourquoi les systèmes d'exploitation existent.\n- Décrire les principales responsabilités d'un système d'exploitation.\n- Distinguer les applications du système d'exploitation.\n- Identifier les principales familles de systèmes d'exploitation.\n- Comprendre les concepts de processus et de threads.\n- Expliquer la mémoire virtuelle à un niveau conceptuel.\n- Comprendre le rôle des API, des appels système et des bibliothèques.\n- Expliquer le but des pilotes de périphériques.\n- Comprendre comment les systèmes de fichiers organisent les données.\n- Distinguer le mode utilisateur du mode noyau.\n\n## Introduction\n\nDans les chapitres précédents, nous avons appris :\n- Le matériel informatique\n- Les instructions machine\n- La programmation\n- Le développement logiciel\n\nUne question naturelle se pose maintenant :\n\n> Comment toutes ces applications logicielles partagent-elles un seul ordinateur ?\n\nConsidérons un ordinateur portable typique.\n\nIl peut exécuter en même temps :\n- Un navigateur web\n- Un lecteur de musique\n- Un traitement de texte\n- Un logiciel antivirus\n- Plusieurs services en arrière-plan\n\nComment est-ce possible ?\n\nLa réponse est le **système d'exploitation**.\n\nLe système d'exploitation agit comme le gestionnaire de l'ordinateur.\n\n## Qu'est-ce qu'un système d'exploitation ?\n\nUn système d'exploitation (SE) est un logiciel qui gère les ressources de l'ordinateur et fournit des services aux applications.\n\nParmi les exemples, on trouve :\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Position dans la pile informatique\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nLes applications interagissent avec le système d'exploitation, qui à son tour interagit avec le matériel.\n\n### Principales responsabilités\n\nUn système d'exploitation gère :\n- Les processeurs\n- La mémoire\n- Les fichiers\n- Le stockage\n- Les connexions réseau\n- Les périphériques\n- La sécurité\n\nSans système d'exploitation, chaque application devrait contrôler directement le matériel.\n\n## La vie sans système d'exploitation\n\nPour apprécier le rôle d'un système d'exploitation, imaginez un ordinateur sans celui-ci.\n\nUn programmeur devrait :\n- Gérer la mémoire manuellement\n- Communiquer directement avec les périphériques\n- Contrôler le matériel de stockage\n- Gérer les opérations du clavier et de l'écran\n- Coordonner plusieurs programmes\n\nChaque application répéterait le même travail.\n\n### Problèmes\n\nSans système d'exploitation :\n- Les logiciels deviendraient extrêmement complexes.\n- Les applications pourraient facilement interférer les unes avec les autres.\n- La sécurité serait médiocre.\n- La compatibilité matérielle serait difficile.\n\n### Analogie\n\nPensez à un grand immeuble de bureaux.\nSans gestion :\n- Les salles sont attribuées au hasard.\n- Les ressources sont partagées de manière inefficace.\n- Des conflits surviennent constamment.\n\nLe système d'exploitation agit comme le gestionnaire de l'immeuble."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"91bc348f917b80f98084c22ed142838e5b2fb908ce35641484b1bd078112aa95","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Operating System Families\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Widely used on personal computers and business environments.\n- Known for broad application support.\n\n### Linux\n- Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.\n- Also available for desktop systems.\n- Linux is open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Developed for Apple computers.\n- Known for tight integration between hardware and software.\n\n### Android\n- Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.\n- Based on Linux technologies.\n\n### iOS\n- Used on Apple mobile devices.\n- Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.\n\n## Resource Management\n\nOne of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.\n\nResources include:\n- CPU time\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Network access\n- Peripheral devices\n\n### Why Resource Management Is Needed\n\nSuppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.\n\nWho gets access?\n\nThe operating system decides.\n\n### Goal\n\nThe OS attempts to:\n- Share resources fairly\n- Maximize performance\n- Prevent conflicts\n\n## Processes\n\nA process is a running instance of a program.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose you open:\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nThe operating system creates a process for Chrome.\n\n### Multiple Processes\n\nYour computer may simultaneously run:\n- Browser process\n- Word processor process\n- Music player process\n- Background operating system processes\n\nEach process has:\n- Its own memory\n- Its own data\n- Its own execution state\n\n### Why Processes Matter\n\nProcesses provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.\n\n## Process Scheduling\n\nMost computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.\n\nTherefore the CPU is shared.\n\n### Scheduling\n\nThe operating system rapidly switches between processes.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nThis occurs extremely quickly.\n\n### User Experience\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nEven though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.\n\n### Analogy\n\nA teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.\n\n## Threads\n\nA process can contain one or more threads.\n\nA thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.\n\n### Example\n\nA web browser may:\n- Display a webpage\n- Download images\n- Play audio\n\nsimultaneously.\n\nThese tasks may be handled by different threads.\n\n### Benefits\n\nThreads improve:\n- Responsiveness\n- Performance\n- Resource utilization\n\n### Analogy\n\nA company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.","output":"","result":"## Operating System Families\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Widely used on personal computers and business environments.\n- Known for broad application support.\n\n### Linux\n- Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.\n- Also available for desktop systems.\n- Linux is open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Developed for Apple computers.\n- Known for tight integration between hardware and software.\n\n### Android\n- Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.\n- Based on Linux technologies.\n\n### iOS\n- Used on Apple mobile devices.\n- Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.\n\n## Resource Management\n\nOne of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.\n\nResources include:\n- CPU time\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Network access\n- Peripheral devices\n\n### Why Resource Management Is Needed\n\nSuppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.\n\nWho gets access?\n\nThe operating system decides.\n\n### Goal\n\nThe OS attempts to:\n- Share resources fairly\n- Maximize performance\n- Prevent conflicts\n\n## Processes\n\nA process is a running instance of a program.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose you open:\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nThe operating system creates a process for Chrome.\n\n### Multiple Processes\n\nYour computer may simultaneously run:\n- Browser process\n- Word processor process\n- Music player process\n- Background operating system processes\n\nEach process has:\n- Its own memory\n- Its own data\n- Its own execution state\n\n### Why Processes Matter\n\nProcesses provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.\n\n## Process Scheduling\n\nMost computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.\n\nTherefore the CPU is shared.\n\n### Scheduling\n\nThe operating system rapidly switches between processes.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nThis occurs extremely quickly.\n\n### User Experience\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nEven though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.\n\n### Analogy\n\nA teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.\n\n## Threads\n\nA process can contain one or more threads.\n\nA thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.\n\n### Example\n\nA web browser may:\n- Display a webpage\n- Download images\n- Play audio\n\nsimultaneously.\n\nThese tasks may be handled by different threads.\n\n### Benefits\n\nThreads improve:\n- Responsiveness\n- Performance\n- Resource utilization\n\n### Analogy\n\nA company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-operating-system-families\">\n<h1>Operating System Families</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-microsoft-windows\">\n<h1>Microsoft Windows</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Widely used on personal computers and business environments.</li>\n<li>Known for broad application support.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-linux\">\n<h1>Linux</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.</li>\n<li>Also available for desktop systems.</li>\n<li>Linux is open source.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-macos\">\n<h1>macOS</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed for Apple computers.</li>\n<li>Known for tight integration between hardware and software.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-android\">\n<h1>Android</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.</li>\n<li>Based on Linux technologies.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-ios\">\n<h1>iOS</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Used on Apple mobile devices.</li>\n<li>Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-resource-management\">\n<h1>Resource Management</h1>\n<p>One of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.</p>\n<p>Resources include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU time</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n<li>Network access</li>\n<li>Peripheral devices</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-why-resource-management-is-needed\">\n<h1>Why Resource Management Is Needed</h1>\n<p>Suppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.</p>\n<p>Who gets access?</p>\n<p>The operating system decides.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-goal\">\n<h1>Goal</h1>\n<p>The OS attempts to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Share resources fairly</li>\n<li>Maximize performance</li>\n<li>Prevent conflicts</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-processes\">\n<h1>Processes</h1>\n<p>A process is a running instance of a program.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Suppose you open:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Google Chrome</pre>\n<p>The operating system creates a process for Chrome.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-multiple-processes\">\n<h1>Multiple Processes</h1>\n<p>Your computer may simultaneously run:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Browser process</li>\n<li>Word processor process</li>\n<li>Music player process</li>\n<li>Background operating system processes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each process has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its own memory</li>\n<li>Its own data</li>\n<li>Its own execution state</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-why-processes-matter\">\n<h1>Why Processes Matter</h1>\n<p>Processes provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-process-scheduling\">\n<h1>Process Scheduling</h1>\n<p>Most computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.</p>\n<p>Therefore the CPU is shared.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-scheduling\">\n<h1>Scheduling</h1>\n<p>The operating system rapidly switches between processes.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Process A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...</pre>\n<p>This occurs extremely quickly.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-user-experience\">\n<h1>User Experience</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Everything running simultaneously.</pre>\n<p>Even though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>A teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-threads\">\n<h1>Threads</h1>\n<p>A process can contain one or more threads.</p>\n<p>A thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>A web browser may:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Display a webpage</li>\n<li>Download images</li>\n<li>Play audio</li>\n</ul>\n<p>simultaneously.</p>\n<p>These tasks may be handled by different threads.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Threads improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Responsiveness</li>\n<li>Performance</li>\n<li>Resource utilization</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-analogy-2\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>A company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"f374a6b56a4a2e2f657f8b4d448ab930f85e48dc1fa4767d21ef28112872f478","markdown":"## Besturingssysteemfamilies\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Veel gebruikt op personal computers en in zakelijke omgevingen.\n- Bekend om brede applicatieondersteuning.\n\n### Linux\n- Veel gebruikt op servers, supercomputers en embedded systemen.\n- Ook beschikbaar voor desktopsystemen.\n- Linux is open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Ontwikkeld voor Apple-computers.\n- Bekend om de nauwe integratie tussen hardware en software.\n\n### Android\n- Voornamelijk gebruikt op smartphones en tablets.\n- Gebaseerd op Linux-technologieën.\n\n### iOS\n- Gebruikt op mobiele apparaten van Apple.\n- Specifiek ontworpen voor smartphones en tablets.\n\n## Resourcebeheer\n\nEen van de belangrijkste verantwoordelijkheden van een besturingssysteem is resourcebeheer.\n\nResources omvatten:\n- CPU-tijd\n- Geheugen\n- Opslag\n- Netwerktoegang\n- Randapparatuur\n\n### Waarom resourcebeheer nodig is\n\nStel dat drie applicaties tegelijkertijd de CPU willen gebruiken.\n\nWie krijgt toegang?\n\nHet besturingssysteem beslist.\n\n### Doel\n\nHet besturingssysteem probeert:\n- Resources eerlijk te verdelen\n- De prestaties te maximaliseren\n- Conflicten te voorkomen\n\n## Processen\n\nEen proces is een draaiende instantie van een programma.\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\nStel dat u het volgende opent:\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nHet besturingssysteem maakt een proces aan voor Chrome.\n\n### Meerdere processen\n\nUw computer kan tegelijkertijd uitvoeren:\n- Browserproces\n- Tekstverwerkerproces\n- Muziekspelerproces\n- Achtergrondprocessen van het besturingssysteem\n\nElk proces heeft:\n- Zijn eigen geheugen\n- Zijn eigen gegevens\n- Zijn eigen uitvoeringsstatus\n\n### Waarom processen belangrijk zijn\n\nProcessen bieden isolatie.\nEen crash in één proces laat meestal niet het hele systeem crashen.\n\n## Procesplanning\n\nDe meeste computers hebben minder CPU-cores dan actieve processen.\n\nDaarom wordt de CPU gedeeld.\n\n### Planning\n\nHet besturingssysteem schakelt snel tussen processen.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nDit gebeurt uiterst snel.\n\n### Gebruikerservaring\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nOok al schakelt de processor voortdurend tussen taken.\n\n### Analogie\n\nEen leraar die vragen beantwoordt van veel leerlingen.\nDe leraar schakelt snel zijn aandacht tussen hen.\n\n## Threads\n\nEen proces kan een of meer threads bevatten.\n\nEen thread vertegenwoordigt een uitvoeringsvolgorde binnen een proces.\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\nEen webbrowser kan:\n- Een webpagina weergeven\n- Afbeeldingen downloaden\n- Audio afspelen\n\ntegelijkertijd.\n\nDeze taken kunnen door verschillende threads worden afgehandeld.\n\n### Voordelen\n\nThreads verbeteren:\n- De responsiviteit\n- De prestaties\n- Het gebruik van resources\n\n### Analogie\n\nEen bedrijf is een proces.\nMedewerkers die aan verschillende taken werken zijn threads."},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"f374a6b56a4a2e2f657f8b4d448ab930f85e48dc1fa4767d21ef28112872f478","markdown":"## Familles de systèmes d'exploitation\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Largement utilisé sur les ordinateurs personnels et dans les environnements professionnels.\n- Reconnu pour sa large prise en charge des applications.\n\n### Linux\n- Couramment utilisé sur les serveurs, les superordinateurs et les systèmes embarqués.\n- Également disponible pour les systèmes de bureau.\n- Linux est open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Développé pour les ordinateurs Apple.\n- Reconnu pour son intégration étroite entre le matériel et le logiciel.\n\n### Android\n- Principalement utilisé sur les smartphones et les tablettes.\n- Basé sur les technologies Linux.\n\n### iOS\n- Utilisé sur les appareils mobiles Apple.\n- Conçu spécifiquement pour les smartphones et les tablettes.\n\n## Gestion des ressources\n\nL'une des responsabilités les plus importantes d'un système d'exploitation est la gestion des ressources.\n\nLes ressources comprennent :\n- Le temps processeur (CPU)\n- La mémoire\n- Le stockage\n- L'accès réseau\n- Les périphériques\n\n### Pourquoi la gestion des ressources est-elle nécessaire\n\nSupposons que trois applications souhaitent utiliser le processeur simultanément.\n\nQui obtient l'accès ?\n\nLe système d'exploitation en décide.\n\n### Objectif\n\nLe système d'exploitation tente de :\n- Partager les ressources équitablement\n- Maximiser les performances\n- Éviter les conflits\n\n## Processus\n\nUn processus est une instance en cours d'exécution d'un programme.\n\n### Exemple\n\nSupposons que vous ouvriez :\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nLe système d'exploitation crée un processus pour Chrome.\n\n### Processus multiples\n\nVotre ordinateur peut exécuter simultanément :\n- Un processus de navigateur\n- Un processus de traitement de texte\n- Un processus de lecteur de musique\n- Des processus d'arrière-plan du système d'exploitation\n\nChaque processus possède :\n- Sa propre mémoire\n- Ses propres données\n- Son propre état d'exécution\n\n### Pourquoi les processus sont importants\n\nLes processus assurent l'isolation.\nUn plantage dans un processus n'entraîne généralement pas le plantage de l'ensemble du système.\n\n## Ordonnancement des processus\n\nLa plupart des ordinateurs disposent de moins de cœurs processeurs que de processus actifs.\n\nLe processeur est donc partagé.\n\n### Ordonnancement\n\nLe système d'exploitation bascule rapidement entre les processus.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nCela se produit extrêmement rapidement.\n\n### Expérience utilisateur\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nMême si le processeur bascule constamment entre les tâches.\n\n### Analogie\n\nUn enseignant répondant aux questions de nombreux élèves.\nL'enseignant déplace rapidement son attention de l'un à l'autre.\n\n## Threads\n\nUn processus peut contenir un ou plusieurs threads.\n\nUn thread représente une séquence d'exécution au sein d'un processus.\n\n### Exemple\n\nUn navigateur web peut :\n- Afficher une page web\n- Télécharger des images\n- Lire de l'audio\n\nsimultanément.\n\nCes tâches peuvent être prises en charge par différents threads.\n\n### Avantages\n\nLes threads améliorent :\n- La réactivité\n- Les performances\n- L'utilisation des ressources\n\n### Analogie\n\nUne entreprise est un processus.\nLes employés travaillant sur différentes tâches sont des threads."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"f374a6b56a4a2e2f657f8b4d448ab930f85e48dc1fa4767d21ef28112872f478","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Memory Management\n\nPrograms need memory to store:\n- Variables\n- Documents\n- Images\n- Running code\n\nThe operating system manages this memory.\n\n### Challenges\n\nThe OS must ensure:\n- Programs receive memory.\n- Programs do not overwrite each other's memory.\n- Memory is efficiently utilized.\n\n### Protection\n\nApplications should not access memory belonging to other applications.\n\nThis prevents:\n- Crashes\n- Data corruption\n- Security problems\n\n## Virtual Memory\n\nModern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.\n\nVirtual memory helps solve this problem.\n\n### Basic Idea\n\nApplications believe they have access to a very large memory space.\n\nThe operating system maps this virtual memory onto:\n- RAM\n- Storage\n\nas needed.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nThe operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.\n\n### Benefits\n\nVirtual memory provides:\n- Flexibility\n- Isolation\n- Simplified programming","output":"","result":"## Memory Management\n\nPrograms need memory to store:\n- Variables\n- Documents\n- Images\n- Running code\n\nThe operating system manages this memory.\n\n### Challenges\n\nThe OS must ensure:\n- Programs receive memory.\n- Programs do not overwrite each other's memory.\n- Memory is efficiently utilized.\n\n### Protection\n\nApplications should not access memory belonging to other applications.\n\nThis prevents:\n- Crashes\n- Data corruption\n- Security problems\n\n## Virtual Memory\n\nModern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.\n\nVirtual memory helps solve this problem.\n\n### Basic Idea\n\nApplications believe they have access to a very large memory space.\n\nThe operating system maps this virtual memory onto:\n- RAM\n- Storage\n\nas needed.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nThe operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.\n\n### Benefits\n\nVirtual memory provides:\n- Flexibility\n- Isolation\n- Simplified programming","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-memory-management\">\n<h1>Memory Management</h1>\n<p>Programs need memory to store:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Variables</li>\n<li>Documents</li>\n<li>Images</li>\n<li>Running code</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The operating system manages this memory.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-challenges\">\n<h1>Challenges</h1>\n<p>The OS must ensure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Programs receive memory.</li>\n<li>Programs do not overwrite each other’s memory.</li>\n<li>Memory is efficiently utilized.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-protection\">\n<h1>Protection</h1>\n<p>Applications should not access memory belonging to other applications.</p>\n<p>This prevents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Crashes</li>\n<li>Data corruption</li>\n<li>Security problems</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-virtual-memory\">\n<h1>Virtual Memory</h1>\n<p>Modern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.</p>\n<p>Virtual memory helps solve this problem.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-basic-idea\">\n<h1>Basic Idea</h1>\n<p>Applications believe they have access to a very large memory space.</p>\n<p>The operating system maps this virtual memory onto:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RAM</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>as needed.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">RAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB</pre>\n<p>The operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Virtual memory provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexibility</li>\n<li>Isolation</li>\n<li>Simplified programming</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"333aeea8e11fad1d31b2cf46342d1e7d25fb50de33e29566bd91e09e8585a334","markdown":"## Geheugenbeheer\n\nProgramma's hebben geheugen nodig om op te slaan:\n- Variabelen\n- Documenten\n- Afbeeldingen\n- Draaiende code\n\nHet besturingssysteem beheert dit geheugen.\n\n### Uitdagingen\n\nHet besturingssysteem moet ervoor zorgen dat:\n- Programma's geheugen krijgen.\n- Programma's elkaars geheugen niet overschrijven.\n- Geheugen efficiënt wordt gebruikt.\n\n### Bescherming\n\nToepassingen mogen geen toegang hebben tot geheugen dat aan andere toepassingen toebehoort.\n\nDit voorkomt:\n- Crashes\n- Gegevenscorruptie\n- Beveiligingsproblemen\n\n## Virtueel Geheugen\n\nModerne computers voeren vaak programma's uit die meer geheugen vereisen dan er fysiek beschikbaar is.\n\nVirtueel geheugen helpt dit probleem op te lossen.\n\n### Basisidee\n\nToepassingen denken dat ze toegang hebben tot een zeer groot geheugen.\n\nHet besturingssysteem koppelt dit virtuele geheugen aan:\n- RAM\n- Opslag\n\nnaar behoefte.\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nHet besturingssysteem kan minder gebruikte informatie tijdelijk op opslag plaatsen.\n\n### Voordelen\n\nVirtueel geheugen biedt:\n- Flexibiliteit\n- Isolatie\n- Vereenvoudigde programmering"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"333aeea8e11fad1d31b2cf46342d1e7d25fb50de33e29566bd91e09e8585a334","markdown":"## Gestion de la mémoire\n\nLes programmes ont besoin de mémoire pour stocker :\n- Des variables\n- Des documents\n- Des images\n- Du code en cours d'exécution\n\nLe système d'exploitation gère cette mémoire.\n\n### Défis\n\nLe système d'exploitation doit garantir que :\n- Les programmes reçoivent de la mémoire.\n- Les programmes n'écrasent pas la mémoire les uns des autres.\n- La mémoire est utilisée efficacement.\n\n### Protection\n\nLes applications ne devraient pas accéder à la mémoire appartenant à d'autres applications.\n\nCela évite :\n- Les plantages\n- La corruption des données\n- Les problèmes de sécurité\n\n## Mémoire virtuelle\n\nLes ordinateurs modernes exécutent souvent des programmes nécessitant plus de mémoire que ce qui est physiquement disponible.\n\nLa mémoire virtuelle aide à résoudre ce problème.\n\n### Idée de base\n\nLes applications pensent avoir accès à un espace mémoire très vaste.\n\nLe système d'exploitation associe cette mémoire virtuelle à :\n- La RAM\n- Le stockage\n\nselon les besoins.\n\n### Exemple\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nLe système d'exploitation peut stocker temporairement les informations les moins utilisées sur le stockage.\n\n### Avantages\n\nLa mémoire virtuelle offre :\n- De la flexibilité\n- De l'isolation\n- Une programmation simplifiée"}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"333aeea8e11fad1d31b2cf46342d1e7d25fb50de33e29566bd91e09e8585a334","language":"English"}},{"input":"## User Mode and Kernel Mode\n\nModern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.\n\n### User Mode\n\nMost applications run in:\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nRestrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Control hardware\n- Access protected memory\n- Modify operating system internals\n\n### Kernel Mode\n\nThe operating system's core runs in:\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nThis mode has full access to the system.\n\n### Why Separation Matters\n\nOne software bug could:\n- Corrupt data\n- Damage system stability\n- Create major security risks\n\nMode separation helps prevent such problems.\n\n## Libraries\n\nApplications often require common functionality.\n\nExamples:\n- Displaying windows\n- Connecting to networks\n- Reading files\n\nRather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\nLibraries improve:\n- Productivity\n- Reliability\n- Reusability\n\n## System Calls\n\nApplications occasionally need operating system services.\n\nThey request these services through system calls.\n\n### Examples\n\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Read a file from storage\n- Allocate memory\n- Access hardware\n\nInstead they ask the operating system.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)\n\nAn API is a defined way for software components to communicate.\n\n### Purpose\n\nAPIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Simplicity\n- Portability\n- Modularity\n\n## Device Drivers\n\nComputers interact with many hardware devices.\n\nExamples:\n- Printers\n- Keyboards\n- Screens\n- Cameras\n- Network cards\n\nA device driver serves as a translator between:\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## File Systems\n\nData must be organized efficiently.\n\nThe file system provides this organization.\n\n### Responsibilities\n\n- Files\n- Directories (folders)\n- File names\n- Storage locations\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services and Background Processes\n\nNot all software is visible to users.\n\nExamples:\n- Security software\n- Networking services\n- Cloud synchronization\n- Software update services\n\nThese services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.","output":"","result":"## User Mode and Kernel Mode\n\nModern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.\n\n### User Mode\n\nMost applications run in:\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nRestrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Control hardware\n- Access protected memory\n- Modify operating system internals\n\n### Kernel Mode\n\nThe operating system's core runs in:\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nThis mode has full access to the system.\n\n### Why Separation Matters\n\nOne software bug could:\n- Corrupt data\n- Damage system stability\n- Create major security risks\n\nMode separation helps prevent such problems.\n\n## Libraries\n\nApplications often require common functionality.\n\nExamples:\n- Displaying windows\n- Connecting to networks\n- Reading files\n\nRather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\nLibraries improve:\n- Productivity\n- Reliability\n- Reusability\n\n## System Calls\n\nApplications occasionally need operating system services.\n\nThey request these services through system calls.\n\n### Examples\n\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Read a file from storage\n- Allocate memory\n- Access hardware\n\nInstead they ask the operating system.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)\n\nAn API is a defined way for software components to communicate.\n\n### Purpose\n\nAPIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Simplicity\n- Portability\n- Modularity\n\n## Device Drivers\n\nComputers interact with many hardware devices.\n\nExamples:\n- Printers\n- Keyboards\n- Screens\n- Cameras\n- Network cards\n\nA device driver serves as a translator between:\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## File Systems\n\nData must be organized efficiently.\n\nThe file system provides this organization.\n\n### Responsibilities\n\n- Files\n- Directories (folders)\n- File names\n- Storage locations\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services and Background Processes\n\nNot all software is visible to users.\n\nExamples:\n- Security software\n- Networking services\n- Cloud synchronization\n- Software update services\n\nThese services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-user-mode-and-kernel-mode\">\n<h1>User Mode and Kernel Mode</h1>\n<p>Modern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-user-mode\">\n<h1>User Mode</h1>\n<p>Most applications run in:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">User Mode</pre>\n<p>Restrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control hardware</li>\n<li>Access protected memory</li>\n<li>Modify operating system internals</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-kernel-mode\">\n<h1>Kernel Mode</h1>\n<p>The operating system’s core runs in:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Kernel Mode</pre>\n<p>This mode has full access to the system.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-why-separation-matters\">\n<h1>Why Separation Matters</h1>\n<p>One software bug could:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corrupt data</li>\n<li>Damage system stability</li>\n<li>Create major security risks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mode separation helps prevent such problems.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-libraries\">\n<h1>Libraries</h1>\n<p>Applications often require common functionality.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Displaying windows</li>\n<li>Connecting to networks</li>\n<li>Reading files</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Open File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Libraries improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Productivity</li>\n<li>Reliability</li>\n<li>Reusability</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-system-calls\">\n<h1>System Calls</h1>\n<p>Applications occasionally need operating system services.</p>\n<p>They request these services through system calls.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-examples\">\n<h1>Examples</h1>\n<p>Applications cannot directly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read a file from storage</li>\n<li>Allocate memory</li>\n<li>Access hardware</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Instead they ask the operating system.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Application\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-application-programming-interfaces-apis\">\n<h1>Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)</h1>\n<p>An API is a defined way for software components to communicate.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-purpose\">\n<h1>Purpose</h1>\n<p>APIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-benefits-2\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Simplicity</li>\n<li>Portability</li>\n<li>Modularity</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-device-drivers\">\n<h1>Device Drivers</h1>\n<p>Computers interact with many hardware devices.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Printers</li>\n<li>Keyboards</li>\n<li>Screens</li>\n<li>Cameras</li>\n<li>Network cards</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A device driver serves as a translator between:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Operating System\n↓\nHardware Device</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-file-systems\">\n<h1>File Systems</h1>\n<p>Data must be organized efficiently.</p>\n<p>The file system provides this organization.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Responsibilities</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Files</li>\n<li>Directories (folders)</li>\n<li>File names</li>\n<li>Storage locations</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Documents\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-services-and-background-processes\">\n<h1>Services and Background Processes</h1>\n<p>Not all software is visible to users.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security software</li>\n<li>Networking services</li>\n<li>Cloud synchronization</li>\n<li>Software update services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"418d2f01c6b005444136930c9471b9f392bc443ebb6daad5ac4e0c6df4931036","markdown":"## Gebruikersmodus en Kernelmodus\n\nModerne besturingssystemen verdelen software in twee uitvoeringsmodi.\n\n### Gebruikersmodus\n\nDe meeste toepassingen draaien in:\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nEr gelden beperkingen.\nToepassingen kunnen niet rechtstreeks:\n- Hardware aansturen\n- Toegang krijgen tot beveiligd geheugen\n- Interne onderdelen van het besturingssysteem wijzigen\n\n### Kernelmodus\n\nDe kern van het besturingssysteem draait in:\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nDeze modus heeft volledige toegang tot het systeem.\n\n### Waarom scheiding belangrijk is\n\nEén softwarefout kan:\n- Gegevens beschadigen\n- De systeemstabiliteit schaden\n- Grote veiligheidsrisico's veroorzaken\n\nModusscheiding helpt dergelijke problemen te voorkomen.\n\n## Bibliotheken\n\nToepassingen hebben vaak gemeenschappelijke functionaliteit nodig.\n\nVoorbeelden:\n- Vensters weergeven\n- Verbinding maken met netwerken\n- Bestanden lezen\n\nIn plaats van deze functionaliteit steeds opnieuw te schrijven, gebruiken ontwikkelaars bibliotheken.\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Voordelen\n\nBibliotheken verbeteren:\n- Productiviteit\n- Betrouwbaarheid\n- Herbruikbaarheid\n\n## Systeemaanroepen\n\nToepassingen hebben soms diensten van het besturingssysteem nodig.\n\nZij vragen deze diensten aan via systeemaanroepen.\n\n### Voorbeelden\n\nToepassingen kunnen niet rechtstreeks:\n- Een bestand van opslag lezen\n- Geheugen toewijzen\n- Toegang krijgen tot hardware\n\nIn plaats daarvan vragen ze dit aan het besturingssysteem.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Application Programming Interfaces (API's)\n\nEen API is een vastgelegde manier waarop softwarecomponenten met elkaar communiceren.\n\n### Doel\n\nAPI's stellen toepassingen in staat om diensten te gebruiken zonder de interne implementatiedetails te kennen.\n\n### Voordelen\n\n- Eenvoud\n- Portabiliteit\n- Modulariteit\n\n## Apparaatstuurprogramma's\n\nComputers werken samen met veel hardwareapparaten.\n\nVoorbeelden:\n- Printers\n- Toetsenborden\n- Schermen\n- Camera's\n- Netwerkkaarten\n\nEen apparaatstuurprogramma fungeert als vertaler tussen:\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## Bestandssystemen\n\nGegevens moeten efficiënt worden georganiseerd.\n\nHet bestandssysteem zorgt voor deze organisatie.\n\n### Verantwoordelijkheden\n\n- Bestanden\n- Mappen (directory's)\n- Bestandsnamen\n- Opslaglocaties\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services en achtergrondprocessen\n\nNiet alle software is zichtbaar voor gebruikers.\n\nVoorbeelden:\n- Beveiligingssoftware\n- Netwerkdiensten\n- Cloudsynchronisatie\n- Softwareupdatediensten\n\nDeze services voeren ondersteunende taken uit terwijl gebruikers met toepassingen werken."},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"418d2f01c6b005444136930c9471b9f392bc443ebb6daad5ac4e0c6df4931036","markdown":"## Mode utilisateur et mode noyau\n\nLes systèmes d'exploitation modernes séparent les logiciels en deux modes d'exécution.\n\n### Mode utilisateur\n\nLa plupart des applications s'exécutent en :\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nDes restrictions s'appliquent.\nLes applications ne peuvent pas directement :\n- Contrôler le matériel\n- Accéder à la mémoire protégée\n- Modifier les composants internes du système d'exploitation\n\n### Mode noyau\n\nLe cœur du système d'exploitation s'exécute en :\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nCe mode a un accès complet au système.\n\n### Pourquoi la séparation est importante\n\nUn seul bogue logiciel pourrait :\n- Corrompre des données\n- Compromettre la stabilité du système\n- Créer des risques de sécurité majeurs\n\nLa séparation des modes aide à prévenir de tels problèmes.\n\n## Bibliothèques\n\nLes applications ont souvent besoin de fonctionnalités communes.\n\nExemples :\n- Afficher des fenêtres\n- Se connecter à des réseaux\n- Lire des fichiers\n\nPlutôt que de réécrire ces fonctionnalités à répétition, les développeurs utilisent des bibliothèques.\n\n### Exemple\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Avantages\n\nLes bibliothèques améliorent :\n- La productivité\n- La fiabilité\n- La réutilisabilité\n\n## Appels système\n\nLes applications ont parfois besoin de services du système d'exploitation.\n\nElles demandent ces services par le biais d'appels système.\n\n### Exemples\n\nLes applications ne peuvent pas directement :\n- Lire un fichier depuis le stockage\n- Allouer de la mémoire\n- Accéder au matériel\n\nElles le demandent plutôt au système d'exploitation.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Interfaces de programmation d'applications (API)\n\nUne API est une méthode définie permettant aux composants logiciels de communiquer.\n\n### Objectif\n\nLes API permettent aux applications d'utiliser des services sans connaître les détails de leur implémentation interne.\n\n### Avantages\n\n- Simplicité\n- Portabilité\n- Modularité\n\n## Pilotes de périphériques\n\nLes ordinateurs interagissent avec de nombreux périphériques matériels.\n\nExemples :\n- Imprimantes\n- Claviers\n- Écrans\n- Caméras\n- Cartes réseau\n\nUn pilote de périphérique sert de traducteur entre :\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## Systèmes de fichiers\n\nLes données doivent être organisées efficacement.\n\nLe système de fichiers assure cette organisation.\n\n### Responsabilités\n\n- Fichiers\n- Répertoires (dossiers)\n- Noms de fichiers\n- Emplacements de stockage\n\n### Exemple\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services et processus en arrière-plan\n\nTous les logiciels ne sont pas visibles pour les utilisateurs.\n\nExemples :\n- Logiciels de sécurité\n- Services réseau\n- Synchronisation dans le cloud\n- Services de mise à jour logicielle\n\nCes services effectuent des tâches de support pendant que les utilisateurs travaillent avec des applications."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"418d2f01c6b005444136930c9471b9f392bc443ebb6daad5ac4e0c6df4931036","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Summary\n\n- The operating system manages computer resources.\n- The OS sits between applications and hardware.\n- Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.\n- Processes represent running programs.\n- Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.\n- Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.\n- Memory management allocates and protects memory.\n- Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.\n- User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.\n- Libraries provide reusable functionality.\n- System calls allow applications to request operating system services.\n- APIs define interfaces between software components.\n- Device drivers enable communication with hardware.\n- File systems organize and manage stored data.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Operating System (OS) | Software that manages hardware and provides services |\n| Process | Running instance of a program |\n| Thread | Execution path within a process |\n| Scheduling | Allocation of CPU time among processes |\n| Virtual Memory | Abstraction that extends usable memory |\n| User Mode | Restricted execution mode for applications |\n| Kernel Mode | Privileged execution mode for the operating system |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software functions |\n| System Call | Request from an application to the operating system |\n| API | Interface enabling software communication |\n| Device Driver | Software translating between OS and hardware |\n| File System | Organization of files and folders on storage |\n| Service | Background software providing system functionality |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is the purpose of an operating system?\n- Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?\n- Name five major operating system families.\n- What is a process?\n- What is the difference between a process and a thread?\n- Why is process scheduling necessary?\n- What is virtual memory?\n- Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?\n- What is the purpose of a system call?\n- What is an API?\n- Why are device drivers necessary?\n- What does a file system do?\n- Explain what happens when a document is opened.\n- Why are background services important?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 8\n\nSo far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study **computer networks and the Internet**, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.","output":"","result":"## Summary\n\n- The operating system manages computer resources.\n- The OS sits between applications and hardware.\n- Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.\n- Processes represent running programs.\n- Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.\n- Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.\n- Memory management allocates and protects memory.\n- Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.\n- User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.\n- Libraries provide reusable functionality.\n- System calls allow applications to request operating system services.\n- APIs define interfaces between software components.\n- Device drivers enable communication with hardware.\n- File systems organize and manage stored data.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Operating System (OS) | Software that manages hardware and provides services |\n| Process | Running instance of a program |\n| Thread | Execution path within a process |\n| Scheduling | Allocation of CPU time among processes |\n| Virtual Memory | Abstraction that extends usable memory |\n| User Mode | Restricted execution mode for applications |\n| Kernel Mode | Privileged execution mode for the operating system |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software functions |\n| System Call | Request from an application to the operating system |\n| API | Interface enabling software communication |\n| Device Driver | Software translating between OS and hardware |\n| File System | Organization of files and folders on storage |\n| Service | Background software providing system functionality |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is the purpose of an operating system?\n- Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?\n- Name five major operating system families.\n- What is a process?\n- What is the difference between a process and a thread?\n- Why is process scheduling necessary?\n- What is virtual memory?\n- Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?\n- What is the purpose of a system call?\n- What is an API?\n- Why are device drivers necessary?\n- What does a file system do?\n- Explain what happens when a document is opened.\n- Why are background services important?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 8\n\nSo far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study **computer networks and the Internet**, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>The operating system manages computer resources.</li>\n<li>The OS sits between applications and hardware.</li>\n<li>Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.</li>\n<li>Processes represent running programs.</li>\n<li>Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.</li>\n<li>Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.</li>\n<li>Memory management allocates and protects memory.</li>\n<li>Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.</li>\n<li>User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.</li>\n<li>Libraries provide reusable functionality.</li>\n<li>System calls allow applications to request operating system services.</li>\n<li>APIs define interfaces between software components.</li>\n<li>Device drivers enable communication with hardware.</li>\n<li>File systems organize and manage stored data.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating System (OS)</td>\n<td>Software that manages hardware and provides services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Process</td>\n<td>Running instance of a program</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thread</td>\n<td>Execution path within a process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scheduling</td>\n<td>Allocation of CPU time among processes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Virtual Memory</td>\n<td>Abstraction that extends usable memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User Mode</td>\n<td>Restricted execution mode for applications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kernel Mode</td>\n<td>Privileged execution mode for the operating system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Library</td>\n<td>Reusable collection of software functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Call</td>\n<td>Request from an application to the operating system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API</td>\n<td>Interface enabling software communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Device Driver</td>\n<td>Software translating between OS and hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File System</td>\n<td>Organization of files and folders on storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service</td>\n<td>Background software providing system functionality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the purpose of an operating system?</li>\n<li>Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?</li>\n<li>Name five major operating system families.</li>\n<li>What is a process?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between a process and a thread?</li>\n<li>Why is process scheduling necessary?</li>\n<li>What is virtual memory?</li>\n<li>Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?</li>\n<li>What is the purpose of a system call?</li>\n<li>What is an API?</li>\n<li>Why are device drivers necessary?</li>\n<li>What does a file system do?</li>\n<li>Explain what happens when a document is opened.</li>\n<li>Why are background services important?</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-transition-to-lecture-8\">\n<h1>Transition to Lecture 8</h1>\n<p>So far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.</p>\n<p>In the next lecture, we will study <strong>computer networks and the Internet</strong>, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7afd1da5adb23842301202d3bca7789810826a8395d13e560623be372cc15d62","markdown":"## Samenvatting\n\n- Het besturingssysteem beheert de computerbronnen.\n- Het besturingssysteem bevindt zich tussen toepassingen en hardware.\n- Belangrijke families van besturingssystemen zijn Windows, Linux, macOS, Android en iOS.\n- Processen vertegenwoordigen lopende programma's.\n- Threads bieden meerdere uitvoeringspaden binnen een proces.\n- Scheduling maakt het mogelijk dat veel programma's de CPU delen.\n- Geheugenbeheer wijst geheugen toe en beschermt het.\n- Virtueel geheugen geeft programma's de illusie van grote geheugenruimtes.\n- Gebruikersmodus en kernelmodus verbeteren stabiliteit en veiligheid.\n- Bibliotheken bieden herbruikbare functionaliteit.\n- Systeemaanroepen stellen toepassingen in staat diensten van het besturingssysteem op te vragen.\n- API's definiëren interfaces tussen softwarecomponenten.\n- Stuurprogramma's (device drivers) maken communicatie met hardware mogelijk.\n- Bestandssystemen organiseren en beheren opgeslagen gegevens.\n\n## Kernbegrippen\n\n| Term | Definitie |\n|---|---|\n| Besturingssysteem (OS) | Software die hardware beheert en diensten levert |\n| Proces | Lopend exemplaar van een programma |\n| Thread | Uitvoeringspad binnen een proces |\n| Scheduling | Toewijzing van CPU-tijd aan processen |\n| Virtueel Geheugen | Abstractie die het bruikbare geheugen uitbreidt |\n| Gebruikersmodus | Beperkte uitvoeringsmodus voor toepassingen |\n| Kernelmodus | Bevoorrechte uitvoeringsmodus voor het besturingssysteem |\n| Bibliotheek | Herbruikbare verzameling softwarefuncties |\n| Systeemaanroep | Verzoek van een toepassing aan het besturingssysteem |\n| API | Interface die softwarecommunicatie mogelijk maakt |\n| Stuurprogramma | Software die vertaalt tussen OS en hardware |\n| Bestandssysteem | Organisatie van bestanden en mappen op opslag |\n| Service | Achtergrondsoftware die systeemfunctionaliteit biedt |\n\n## Herhalingsvragen\n\n- Wat is het doel van een besturingssysteem?\n- Waarom zou programmeren moeilijk zijn zonder besturingssysteem?\n- Noem vijf belangrijke families van besturingssystemen.\n- Wat is een proces?\n- Wat is het verschil tussen een proces en een thread?\n- Waarom is procesplanning (scheduling) noodzakelijk?\n- Wat is virtueel geheugen?\n- Waarom zijn gebruikersmodus en kernelmodus gescheiden?\n- Wat is het doel van een systeemaanroep?\n- Wat is een API?\n- Waarom zijn stuurprogramma's noodzakelijk?\n- Wat doet een bestandssysteem?\n- Leg uit wat er gebeurt wanneer een document wordt geopend.\n- Waarom zijn achtergronddiensten belangrijk?\n\n## Overgang naar Les 8\n\nTot nu toe hebben we onderzocht hoe een enkele computer werkt en hoe het besturingssysteem ervan bronnen beheert. Moderne informatica vindt echter zelden in isolatie plaats.\n\nIn de volgende les bestuderen we **computernetwerken en het internet**, en leren we hoe computers communiceren, informatie uitwisselen en diensten over de hele wereld leveren."},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7afd1da5adb23842301202d3bca7789810826a8395d13e560623be372cc15d62","markdown":"## Résumé\n\n- Le système d'exploitation gère les ressources de l'ordinateur.\n- Le système d'exploitation se situe entre les applications et le matériel.\n- Les principales familles de systèmes d'exploitation comprennent Windows, Linux, macOS, Android et iOS.\n- Les processus représentent des programmes en cours d'exécution.\n- Les threads fournissent plusieurs chemins d'exécution au sein d'un processus.\n- L'ordonnancement permet à de nombreux programmes de partager le processeur.\n- La gestion de la mémoire alloue et protège la mémoire.\n- La mémoire virtuelle donne aux programmes l'illusion de disposer de grands espaces mémoire.\n- Le mode utilisateur et le mode noyau améliorent la stabilité et la sécurité.\n- Les bibliothèques fournissent des fonctionnalités réutilisables.\n- Les appels système permettent aux applications de demander des services au système d'exploitation.\n- Les API définissent des interfaces entre les composants logiciels.\n- Les pilotes de périphériques permettent la communication avec le matériel.\n- Les systèmes de fichiers organisent et gèrent les données stockées.\n\n## Termes clés\n\n| Terme | Définition |\n|---|---|\n| Système d'exploitation (SE) | Logiciel qui gère le matériel et fournit des services |\n| Processus | Instance en cours d'exécution d'un programme |\n| Thread | Chemin d'exécution au sein d'un processus |\n| Ordonnancement | Allocation du temps processeur entre les processus |\n| Mémoire virtuelle | Abstraction qui étend la mémoire utilisable |\n| Mode utilisateur | Mode d'exécution restreint pour les applications |\n| Mode noyau | Mode d'exécution privilégié pour le système d'exploitation |\n| Bibliothèque | Collection réutilisable de fonctions logicielles |\n| Appel système | Requête d'une application vers le système d'exploitation |\n| API | Interface permettant la communication entre logiciels |\n| Pilote de périphérique | Logiciel assurant la traduction entre le SE et le matériel |\n| Système de fichiers | Organisation des fichiers et dossiers sur le stockage |\n| Service | Logiciel en arrière-plan fournissant une fonctionnalité système |\n\n## Questions de révision\n\n- Quel est le but d'un système d'exploitation ?\n- Pourquoi la programmation serait-elle difficile sans système d'exploitation ?\n- Nommez cinq grandes familles de systèmes d'exploitation.\n- Qu'est-ce qu'un processus ?\n- Quelle est la différence entre un processus et un thread ?\n- Pourquoi l'ordonnancement des processus est-il nécessaire ?\n- Qu'est-ce que la mémoire virtuelle ?\n- Pourquoi le mode utilisateur et le mode noyau sont-ils séparés ?\n- Quel est le but d'un appel système ?\n- Qu'est-ce qu'une API ?\n- Pourquoi les pilotes de périphériques sont-ils nécessaires ?\n- Que fait un système de fichiers ?\n- Expliquez ce qui se passe lorsqu'un document est ouvert.\n- Pourquoi les services en arrière-plan sont-ils importants ?\n\n## Transition vers le cours 8\n\nJusqu'à présent, nous avons exploré le fonctionnement d'un seul ordinateur et la manière dont son système d'exploitation gère les ressources. Cependant, l'informatique moderne se déroule rarement de manière isolée.\n\nDans le prochain cours, nous étudierons **les réseaux informatiques et Internet**, et nous apprendrons comment les ordinateurs communiquent, échangent des informations et fournissent des services à travers le monde."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7afd1da5adb23842301202d3bca7789810826a8395d13e560623be372cc15d62","language":"English"}}]